\textbf{Proof.}
Put \(\varepsilon=\varepsilon_{N_0,K_T,K_D}(\alpha)\). The uniform linearization gives, for every sufficiently large evaluated triple,
\[
 P\{\|Z_r\|_{\infty,\mathcal G}>c_r^\xi+\omega_r/2\}
 \le\alpha-\varepsilon/4,\qquad
 P\{\|Q_r\|_{\infty,\mathcal G}>\omega_r/2\}\le\varepsilon/4, \tag{1}
\]
uniformly in \(P\), while \(\|\mathfrak b_r\|_\infty\le\mathbf1\{r\ge2\}\eta_D(K_D)\). A union bound in the expansion yields
\[
 P\left\{\max_{g\in\mathcal G}|\widehat\theta_r(g)-\theta(g)|
 \le c_r^\xi+\mathbf1\{r\ge2\}\eta_D(K_D)+\omega_r\right\}
 \ge1-\alpha.                                             \tag{2}
\]
The interpolation proposition gives, with \(q_\beta=\min\{\beta+1,2\}\),
\[
 \|\widehat\theta_r-\theta\|_{\infty,\mathcal I}
 \le\max_{g\in\mathcal G}|\widehat\theta_r(g)-\theta(g)|
 +c_{\mathrm{int}}h^{q_\beta},\qquad h^{q_\beta}\le\rho_r. \tag{3}
\]
Projection onto \([0,1]\) cannot increase distance from the survival curve. Linear interpolation of the band endpoints preserves the grid enclosure plus (3), and all branches are measurable and continuous. Thus the radius in the corrected definition implies simultaneous coverage at least \(1-\alpha\), which is stronger than the defining \(1-\alpha-\varepsilon\) constraint. Hence \(\widehat B_r\in\mathcal H_r\).

Clipping endpoints can only shorten the band, so
\[
 w(\widehat B_r)\le2\widetilde c_r^\xi+2\tau_{r,n}
 +2\mathbf1\{r\ge2\}\eta_D(K_D)+2\omega_r
 +2c_{\mathrm{int}}h^{q_\beta}.                           \tag{4}
\]
The grid-quantile theorem gives \(\sup_PE\widetilde c_r^\xi\le C\rho_r\) and \(\tau_{r,n}=o(\rho_r)\); the linearization gives \(\omega_r=o(\rho_r)\); and (3) bounds interpolation by \(C\rho_r\). Taking expectations in (4), and increasing \(a_0\) so all small-order terms are bounded by fixed multiples of \(\rho_r\), proves
\[
 \sup_{P\in\mathfrak P_r}E_Pw(\widehat B_r)
 \le C\{\rho_r+\mathbf1\{r\ge2\}\eta_D(K_D)\}=C\bar\rho_r. \tag{5}
\]
At rank one this is \(C(N_0K_D)^{-1/2}\). At fixed higher rank it is \(O(\rho_r)\) only under the stated \(\eta_D=O(\rho_r)\) condition. \(\square\)

\textbf{Proof.}
At \(r=1\), \(d=0\), and normalization collapse gives \(\theta=s_1\). The corrected construction pools all \(n_D=N_0K_D\) donor-post records before product-limit estimation, so
\[
 \widehat\theta_1=\mathcal L_{\mathcal G}\widehat S_D^{\mathrm{PL}}. \tag{1}
\]
It attaches one Gaussian multiplier to each pooled patient increment and uses the same aggregate-risk denominator. The pooled non-identically censored lemma supplies the product-integral expansion, moment bounds, and covariance consistency under donor-specific censoring. The corrected critical value is
\[
 c_1^\xi=\widetilde c_1^\xi+\tau_{1,n},                  \tag{2}
\]
and the attaining theorem validates the resulting band, including the zero-variance coordinate at time zero. The higher-rank \(\eta_D\) term is absent, and the interpolation proposition supplies the continuous-time allowance. Thus the rank-one branch is exactly the stated pooled Kaplan--Meier estimator and its patient-level martingale-multiplier simultaneous band, up to interpolation. \(\square\)

\textbf{Proof.}
For the algebraic assertion, the affine identity gives, for every period, unit, and follow-up time,
\[
 \left\langle u_p^{\mathrm{HS}}(t),v_m^{\mathrm{HS}}\right\rangle
 =s_p(t)+b_p(t)^\top z_m
 =S^0_{p,m}(t).                                           \tag{1}
\]
Let \(V_D=[v_m^{\mathrm{HS}}:m\in\mathcal D]\).  Donor centering and covariance normalization yield
\[
 \frac1{N_0}V_DV_D^\top
 =\begin{pmatrix}
  1&N_0^{-1}\sum_{m\in\mathcal D}z_m^\top\\
  N_0^{-1}\sum_{m\in\mathcal D}z_m&
  N_0^{-1}\sum_{m\in\mathcal D}z_mz_m^\top
 \end{pmatrix}
 =\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&I_d\end{pmatrix}
 =I_r.                                                     \tag{2}
\]
Consequently \(V_DV_D^\top=N_0I_r\) is invertible, so \(V_D\) has row rank \(r\) and its columns span \(\mathbb R^r\).  In particular,
\[
 a_\star:=V_D^\top(V_DV_D^\top)^{-1}v_\star^{\mathrm{HS}}
 \quad\text{satisfies}\quad
 V_Da_\star=v_\star^{\mathrm{HS}},                       \tag{3}
\]
which proves donor-span inclusion.

Now let \(P\in\mathfrak P_r(N_0,K_T,K_D)\).  The normalized factor-model assumption supplies an all-unit compatible affine representation, and exact centered rank fixes its contrast dimension at \(d=r-1\).  The canonical factorization centers and covariance-normalizes the donor loadings.  The canonical-target-coordinate lemma shows that target-pre compatibility is preserved by this normalization and that the positive pre-period Gram gives the unique shared target coordinate.  Thus the canonical coordinates satisfy all premises used in (1)--(3), including for \(m=\star\).  The cited Han--Shah scope lemma identifies finite-dimensional survival factorization and donor-span inclusion as the structural conditions used by their Theorem~1.  Hence every law in \(\mathfrak P_r\) is a specialization of that structural model.  This is only a one-way inclusion: none of the canonical normalization, exact-rank, spectrum, pre-Gram, smoothness, or loading-envelope restrictions has been attributed to Han and Shah. \(\square\)
